823:\label{sec:trifferent}
825:Recall that a linear subspace $C$ of $\F_3^n$ is called a \textit{linear trifferent code} if for all distinct $x, y, z \in C$ there exists a coordinate $i$ such that $\{x_i, y_i, z_i\} = \F_3$. The maximum size of a linear trifferent code is denoted by $T_L(n)$. 
828:We say that an (affine) $2$-blocking set $S\subseteq \F_3^k$ is \textit{symmetric} if it is of the form $S=\{\vec{0}\}\cup B\cup -B$ for some set $B\subseteq \F_3^k$. So by Lemma~\ref{lem:equivalence_strong_affine}, a set $S\subseteq \F_3^k$ is a symmetric $2$-blocking set if and only if it is of the form $S=\cup_{\ell\in \mathcal{L}}\ell$ for a strong blocking set $\mathcal{L}\subseteq \mathrm{PG}(k-1,3)$.
830:In~\cite{Pohoata2022} it was shown that a linear trifferent code of dimension $k$ in $\F_3^n$ gives rise to a symmetric $2$-blocking set in $\F_3^k$. We prove that this relation goes both ways.
834:Then $C$ is a linear trifferent code in $\F_3^n$ if and only if $\{\vec{0}\}\cup B\cup -B$ is a $2$-blocking set in $\F_3^k$. 
839:For the forward implication, suppose that $\{\vec{0}\}\cup B\cup -B$ is a 2-blocking set. Since $C$ is a linear subspace, we only need to show the trifferent property for triples of distinct vectors $\vec{0},c,c'\in C$. Writing $c=u^\transp G$ and $c'=v^\transp G$, it suffices to show that there is a column $b$ of $G$ such that $\{u^\transp b, v^\transp v\}=\{-1,1\}$. Since $u$ and $v$ are distinct and nonzero, the set $S = \{x\in \F_3^m: u^\transp x=1, v^\transp x=-1\}$ contains an affine subspace of codimension~$2$. Since $\{\vec{0}\}\cup B\cup -B$ is a 2-blocking set it must intersect $S$. Hence, since $S$ does not contain the zero vector, it contains a vector $x\in B\cup -B$. If $x\in B$ we can take $b=x$ and otherwise we can take $b=-x$.
842:For the backward implication, suppose that $C$ is a trifferent code. Let $V\subseteq \F_3^k$ be an affine subspace of codimension~$2$ with $0\not \in V$. It suffices to show that there is a $b\in B$ with $b\in V$ or $-b\in V$. 
