982:\section{Conclusion}
983:In this paper, we established new connections between affine blocking sets, strong blocking sets, and trifferent codes.
984:We obtained new bounds on affine blocking sets which improve the state of the art for bounds on the latter two objects as well. 
985:Moreover, using the recent explicit constructions of strong blocking sets, we gave new explicit constructions of trifferent codes, beating the current bound. 
988:Recall that $b_q(k, s)$ denotes the smallest size of an affine $s$-blocking set in $\F_q^k$. 
989:While we can prove upper bounds on $b_q(k, s)$ that, for any fixed $s$, are only a constant factor away from the lower bound given in~\eqref{eq:blocking_lb_basic}, the problem of determining the asymptotics of $b_q(k, s)$ when $s$ varies with $k$ is wide open. 
995:While we could improve the lower bounds on strong $(s - 1)$-blocking sets, we are unable to improve the lower bounds on affine $s$-blocking sets given in~\eqref{eq:blocking_lb_basic}, for fixed $s$ and $q, k$ large. 
1001:Finally, we proved a lower bound on linear trifferent codes that is asymptotically equal to the best lower bound on trifferent codes. 
1002:Our lower bound is based on the new upper bound on strong blocking sets, obtained by picking a random set of planes through the origin in $\F_q^k$. 
1009:The data on $T_L(n)$ for small $n$, that we computed in Section~\ref{sec:explicit}, suggests that $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty}\log_3(T_L(n))/n = 1/5$, but we are not too confident to make that conjecture. 
1019:In \cite{wx2001}, an explicit construction is given that has dimension at least a linear function of $n$, when $t = O(q^{1/4})$.
