/tmp/arxiv_2301.09457_src/paper_v2_jlms.tex:825:Recall that a linear subspace $C$ of $\F_3^n$ is called a \textit{linear trifferent code} if for all distinct $x, y, z \in C$ there exists a coordinate $i$ such that $\{x_i, y_i, z_i\} = \F_3$. The maximum size of a linear trifferent code is denoted by $T_L(n)$. 
/tmp/arxiv_2301.09457_src/paper_v2_jlms.tex:828:We say that an (affine) $2$-blocking set $S\subseteq \F_3^k$ is \textit{symmetric} if it is of the form $S=\{\vec{0}\}\cup B\cup -B$ for some set $B\subseteq \F_3^k$. So by Lemma~\ref{lem:equivalence_strong_affine}, a set $S\subseteq \F_3^k$ is a symmetric $2$-blocking set if and only if it is of the form $S=\cup_{\ell\in \mathcal{L}}\ell$ for a strong blocking set $\mathcal{L}\subseteq \mathrm{PG}(k-1,3)$.
/tmp/arxiv_2301.09457_src/paper_v2_jlms.tex:830:In~\cite{Pohoata2022} it was shown that a linear trifferent code of dimension $k$ in $\F_3^n$ gives rise to a symmetric $2$-blocking set in $\F_3^k$. We prove that this relation goes both ways.
