Built a native finite-field Track B generator based on the paper’s trifferent-code equivalence ([arXiv paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.09457)).

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2301.09457/gen_2301_09457.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2301.09457/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2301.09457/README.md)
- [Bare oracle transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2301.09457/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2301.09457/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2301.09457/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

All local gates pass. At shipping size:

- Candidate space: `(3^128−1)/2`
- Random guesses: `0/200,000`
- Reference elimination: mean `833,553` exact operations
- Compact Walsh route: `147` operations
- Answer: 384 characters, 128 atoms
- Verifier checks the finite-field constraints directly and never reads `inst["answer"]`

External hardening remains blocked: both completed models solved `easy`, after which OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 total-limit errors. No higher-rung or G9 verdict exists, so the README correctly marks this as not submission-ready. A funded `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` is required to resume:

```bash
python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_2301_09457.py
```
